      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s earliest landmark was Fort Casco, a palisaded fort and trading post erected in 1698 after King William’s War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falmouth%2C_Maine",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Author Stephen King set several of his early short stories, including “Jerusalem’s Lot,” in the rural backdrop of Falmouth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falmouth%2C_Maine",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before European settlement, the Abenaki‑Pennacook people called the area Aucocisco, living along the Presumpscot River and harvesting corn, beans, and squash.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
